---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import BlogTemplate from "../../components/BlogTemplate.astro";

// IMAGES (reuse or adjust later)
import hero from "../../assets/images/metro.png";
import auto from "../../assets/images/3.png";
import cab from "../../assets/images/traffic.jpg";
import CTA from "../../components/CTA.astro";
import FAQ from "../../components/FAQ.astro";

const faqs = [
    {
        question: "What is the fastest way to travel in Delhi?",
        answer: "The Metro is usually the fastest option for long distances because it avoids traffic completely. For shorter distances, autos can sometimes be quicker.",
    },
    {
        question: "When should I use a cab in Delhi?",
        answer: "Cabs are best for late-night travel, airport transfers, or situations where comfort and direct connectivity matter more than speed.",
    },
    {
        question: "Are auto-rickshaws safe and reliable?",
        answer: "Yes, autos are widely used and reliable for short distances. Using app-based options or checking prices beforehand helps make the experience smoother.",
    },
    {
        question: "Should I use only one mode of transport in Delhi?",
        answer: "No. The most efficient way to travel is by combining Metro, autos, and cabs depending on the situation.",
    },
];
---

<BaseLayout title="Metro vs Cab vs Auto in Delhi | Delhi Concierge">
    <BlogTemplate
        title="Metro vs Cab vs Auto in Delhi: When to Use What"
        heroImage={hero}
        breadcrumbs={[
            { label: "Home", href: "/" },
            { label: "Blog", href: "/blog" },
            { label: "Delhi Survival", href: "/blog/delhi-survival-guide" },
            { label: "Metro vs Cab vs Auto" },
        ]}
        gallery={[hero, auto, cab]}
        cta={{
            title: "Move through Delhi with clarity.",
            description:
                "Understand when to use each mode of transport and avoid unnecessary delays.",
            buttonText: "Explore More Guides",
            link: "/blog",
        }}
        finalThought={{
            title: "Final Thought",
            unbiased_opinion:
                "In Delhi, movement isn’t about choosing one option—it’s about knowing when to switch.",
        }}
        date="March 24, 2026"
        author="Shaw"
        readTimeInMinutes={4}
        nextBlog={{
            title1: "Why Delhi Traffic Feels Chaotic",
            description1:
                "Understand the system behind the chaos and move more efficiently.",
            link1: "/blog/transport/delhi-traffic-guide",
            image1: cab,
            category1: "Transport",
            title2: "How to Avoid Getting Overcharged in Delhi",
            description2:
                "Simple habits that help you avoid overpaying and stay in control.",
            link2: "/blog/pricing/avoid-getting-overcharged-delhi",
            image2: auto,
            category2: "Pricing",
        }}
    >
        <article class="blog-content">
            <h2>How Transport Actually Works in Delhi</h2>

            <p>
                Moving around Delhi isn’t about picking one mode of transport
                and sticking to it. The city doesn’t work that way. Distances
                are long, traffic is unpredictable, and the experience of the
                same route can change completely depending on the time of day.
            </p>

            <p>
                What feels like the obvious choice at one moment can quickly
                become inefficient the next. A cab might feel convenient until
                you’re stuck in traffic, while the Metro might feel fast until
                you realize your destination is still a few kilometers away.
            </p>

            <p class="highlight-bar">
                In Delhi, transport works best when you adapt—not when you
                commit to one option.
            </p>

            <p>
                Locals switch constantly between Metro, cabs, and autos
                depending on the situation. Once you understand when each one
                works best, getting around the city becomes much smoother.
            </p>

            <!-- METRO -->

            <h2>The Metro: Your Anti-Traffic System</h2>

            <p>
                The Delhi Metro is the most predictable way to move across the
                city. It avoids traffic entirely, follows fixed timings, and
                connects most major areas efficiently.
            </p>

            <p>
                If your journey involves covering longer distances—especially
                across different parts of Delhi or nearby cities—the Metro
                almost always saves time. What might take 60–90 minutes by road
                during peak hours can often be reduced significantly on the
                train.
            </p>

            <p class="highlight-bar">
                If your destination is more than 5–6 km away, the Metro is
                usually the fastest option.
            </p>

            <p>
                It becomes even more valuable during peak hours (8:00–10:30 AM
                and 5:00–8:30 PM), when road traffic slows down but the Metro
                continues at the same pace.
            </p>

            <p>
                The only limitation is the “last mile.” Most stations are not
                exactly at your destination, which means you’ll usually need
                another mode of transport once you exit.
            </p>

            <!-- CAB -->

            <h2>Cabs: The Comfort Layer</h2>

            <p>
                Cabs offer something the Metro doesn’t—comfort and direct
                connectivity. You get picked up from your location and dropped
                exactly where you need to go, without transitions or walking.
            </p>

            <p>
                This makes them ideal in situations where convenience matters
                more than speed. Late-night travel, airport runs, formal events,
                or simply avoiding heat and pollution are all scenarios where
                cabs become the better choice.
            </p>

            <p class="highlight-bar">
                When the journey needs to feel easy and controlled, a cab is the
                better option.
            </p>

            <p>
                Services like Uber and BluSmart are widely used, with BluSmart
                often preferred for reliability and cleaner vehicles. However,
                pricing can fluctuate depending on demand, especially during
                rain or rush hours.
            </p>

            <p>
                Because of this, it helps to quickly compare prices across apps
                before booking. That small check can save both time and money.
            </p>

            <!-- AUTO -->

            <h2>Auto-Rickshaws: The Flexible Gap-Filler</h2>

            <p>
                Autos sit somewhere in between—they’re not meant for long
                journeys, but they’re extremely effective for short distances.
            </p>

            <p>
                If your destination is within 2–3 km, or if you’re trying to
                cover the distance between a Metro station and your final stop,
                autos often become the fastest option.
            </p>

            <p class="highlight-bar">
                For short distances, autos move faster than cars stuck in
                traffic.
            </p>

            <p>
                Their biggest advantage is flexibility. They can navigate narrow
                lanes, take quicker turns, and move through traffic in ways
                larger vehicles can’t.
            </p>

            <p>
                A practical approach many people use is to check the price on
                apps like Uber Auto or Rapido first. This gives you a reference,
                which you can either book directly or use while speaking to a
                street driver.
            </p>

            <!-- COMBINING -->

            <h2>How Locals Actually Combine These</h2>

            <p>
                The real efficiency in Delhi doesn’t come from choosing one
                mode—it comes from combining them.
            </p>

            <p>
                A typical journey often looks like this: Metro for the main
                distance, followed by an auto for the last mile, and a cab only
                when comfort or timing becomes important.
            </p>

            <p class="highlight-bar">
                Each mode solves a different part of the journey.
            </p>

            <p>
                Once you start thinking this way, travel decisions become
                simpler. You’re no longer trying to find the “best”
                option—you’re just using the right tool at the right time. If
                you want a clearer system for moving across the city without
                second guessing, this
                <a href="/navigate-delhi"> guide to navigating Delhi </a>
                breaks it down step by step.
            </p>

            <!-- DECISION FRAME -->

            <h2>A Simple Way to Decide</h2>

            <p>If you’re unsure what to choose, a simple mental check helps.</p>

            <p>Think in terms of distance, time, and comfort:</p>

            <ul>
                <li>Long distance → Metro</li>
                <li>Short distance → Auto</li>
                <li>Comfort or late night → Cab</li>
            </ul>

            <p class="highlight-bar">
                You don’t need perfect planning—just a quick sense of what fits
                the situation.
            </p>

            <!-- INSIGHT -->

            <h2>The Real Shift</h2>

            <p>
                At first, transport in Delhi can feel overwhelming because there
                are too many choices.
            </p>

            <p>
                But after a few days, you start noticing patterns. You
                understand when traffic builds, which routes feel faster, and
                how different modes behave at different times.
            </p>

            <p class="highlight-bar">
                What feels complicated at first becomes intuitive very quickly.
            </p>

            <p>
                And once that happens, the city stops feeling difficult to
                navigate—and starts feeling surprisingly efficient.
            </p>

            <!-- FAQ -->
            <FAQ faqs={faqs} />
        </article>

        <style>
            /* ========================= */
            /* SIGNATURE HIGHLIGHT */
            /* ========================= */

            .highlight-bar {
                position: relative;
                padding-left: 14px;
                margin: 14px 0;
                font-weight: 500;
                color: var(--text-primary);
            }

            .highlight-bar::before {
                content: "";
                position: absolute;
                left: 0;
                top: 4px;
                width: 4px;
                height: calc(100% - 8px);
                border-radius: 2px;
                background: linear-gradient(180deg, #ff8c40, #ff6a2a);
            }

            .blog-content {
                font-size: 16px;
                line-height: 1.75;
            }

            .blog-content p {
                margin: 0;
            }

            .blog-content > * + * {
                margin-top: 14px;
            }
        </style>
    </BlogTemplate>

    <CTA
        title="Move through Delhi without confusion."
        text="We help you understand how the city actually works—so every decision feels easier."
        buttonText="Explore Services"
        buttonLink="/services"
    />
</BaseLayout>
